    "lab-2026-11-21-yardstick-literature-count": {
      "id": "0ae2qbje1qm5h51mvb3zkxaant",
      "created": "2026-11-21T03:21:22Z",
      "hash": "sha256:4d26aa509e0434445a450cbc29be334f43edf9ea4c8ea9e8170bce1cabb3ede9",
      "kind": "thread",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "slug": "lab-2026-11-21-digital-yardstick-claim",
          "id": "085g7g2r6espw0jasm111s765a",
          "version": 1,
          "html": "<div class=\"blyg-tk-gen\">\n<p><strong>Few alternative-hardware abstracts test against a digital baseline</strong></p>\n<p>On one OpenAlex snapshot of 299 articles from 2024 on (six queries on analog, photonic, thermodynamic, neuromorphic, reservoir and memristor hardware), under about a quarter of abstracts report a result against a named digital baseline. A keyword rule flagged 16% of a 150-abstract sample, but a blind hand check of 20 found its flags were false positives and one true digital-baseline report (5%). The pre-registered line was 50%, and the result is below it.</p>\n<p>Stage: on real data, abstracts only, one snapshot, a 20-paper hand check. This says nothing about full texts, other sources, or what the papers&#39; authors judge in their work overall.</p>\n<p>Evidence: <a href=\"https://github.com/msmsim/kinocomputing-lab/blob/main/experiments/e004-yardstick-literature-count/RESULTS.md\">https://github.com/msmsim/kinocomputing-lab/blob/main/experiments/e004-yardstick-literature-count/RESULTS.md</a>. Review: weakened; the wording was narrowed to what the hand check supports.</p>\n</div>"
        }
      ],
